<!--
  Garfin syncs nothing to anyone's cloud. See docs/DECISIONS.md § Nothing
  syncs to a cloud account, and issue #35.

  `android:allowBackup="false"` on its own is NOT enough, which is the whole
  reason this file exists. Android's own documentation:

      "For apps targeting Android 12 (API level 31) or higher, this behavior
       varies. On devices from some device manufacturers, specifying
       android:allowBackup="false" disables cloud-based backup and restore
       (such as Google Drive backups) but doesn't disable device-to-device
       transfers for the app."

  Garfin targets well past 31, so that gap is ours, and it is manufacturer-
  dependent — the worst kind, because it would hold on whatever handset it was
  checked on and not on someone else's.

  Two things about the format that are easy to get wrong, both from the same
  docs:

  - `path` is REQUIRED on every rule. A rule without it is not a broader rule,
    it is an invalid one.
  - An EMPTY section means the mode is FULLY ENABLED, not disabled: "If there
    are no rules for a particular backup mode, such as if the <device-transfer>
    section is missing, that mode is fully enabled for all content except for
    no-backup and cache directories." So `<device-transfer />` would be the
    exact opposite of what it looks like. Each section must carry real excludes.

  `root` is the app's private credential-protected directory, which is where
  shared_preferences lives. `device_root` is device-protected storage, a
  separate location that `root` does not cover — nothing writes there today,
  and it is excluded so that stays true by default rather than by remembering.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_root" path="." />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_root" path="." />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
